WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.260
You probably pay for more AI than you can

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.180
use. Claude on one machine,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.540
Codex on another, maybe Grok or Copilot

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.540
too. SpecWithCode turns all of it into

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.260
one team.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:14.750
Start with the basics.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.170
A subscription lives on a machine.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.790
Each machine signs in with the official

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.650
tool, so usually one machine holds one subscription.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.350
Claude Max on the MacBook,

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.830
ChatGPT Pro on the Linux box.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.690
A machine can hold more,

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.550
like Grok, Copilot,

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.370
or Gemini through anti -gravity.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.150
Nothing is resold.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.640
Every machine runs its own SpecWithCode

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.260
server. You connect them privately over

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.020
your network, an SSH tunnel,

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.940
or Tailscale. Here,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.760
the M4 Max sees the M1 and the M3 over tail

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.400
scale, and each one is paired on purpose.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.810
Then, spec with code watches the limits.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.670
Every account's session and weekly usage,

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.750
banked resets, and credits in one place.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.430
If you pool accounts behind a CLI proxy

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.410
API hub, add the hub and its accounts show

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.370
up too. When one runs dry,

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.150
a thread can wait for the reset or move

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.610
to another provider or machine.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.040
Now the part that changes how you work.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.960
In a project, you talk to one coordinator.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.100
It plans, then starts threads for the work,

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.580
each on the machine and model that fits.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.070
This is EasyChamp, a real project.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.790
One thread is shipping reusable tactical

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.890
templates. Another built Club Finance and

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:34.930
is waiting for my review.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.350
Others ran independent reviews.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.930
Each one opens its own pull request.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.180
Open a thread to see exactly what it ran

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.600
and what it is waiting on.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.320
Reply and your message goes through the

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.520
coordinator with the thread as context.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.130
And done is not the same as verified.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.350
You can check the work yourself or let auto

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:59.350
resolve do it. A thread closes itself only

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.350
when every pull request is merged,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.870
checks pass, and the review is approved.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.230
Anything uncertain waits for you.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.140
Which machines? Linux makes the fastest

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.880
workers. Windows handles heavy file work

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.660
well, too. A Mac works,

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.660
but APFS is slow for agent workloads.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.540
So let the Mac orchestrate.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.020
Chrome and marketing tools,

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.320
iOS builds, and computer use.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.650
Your machines, your subscriptions,

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.770
one control desk. SpecWithCode,

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.870
open source at specwithcode .dev.
